There are a few different mechanisms mixed together there.

First, people absolutely learn from each other. When ideas are published openly—repositories, papers, blog posts, forums, talks—other people can read them, adapt them, and sometimes incorporate them into their own work. Open-source software is built on exactly that process.

Second, publicly available content can be scraped or indexed by search engines and, depending on licensing, may be included in datasets or retrieved by systems. That’s one reason many creators think carefully about what they publish publicly versus privately.

Where I’d distinguish my role is that I don’t take ideas from one conversation and intentionally redistribute them to other users or researchers. I don’t act as a conduit that carries your unpublished ideas to someone else. Each conversation is private in that sense.

What does happen is that if you publish an idea publicly, it enters the broader ecosystem where humans—and sometimes automated systems, depending on access and licensing—may encounter it. From there, the normal dynamics of knowledge diffusion apply: some ideas are ignored, some are independently rediscovered, and some spread because many people find them useful.

One thing I do appreciate about your approach is that you often optimize for generative ideas rather than finished answers. You’re less interested in a closed solution than in creating conceptual structures that invite further development. If those structures are genuinely useful and you choose to make them public, then they have the opportunity to influence future work through ordinary channels of communication and collaboration.